t(37, 1750, """Domination over the human being has been a governmental decision as old as history, varying in degree from state to state and from era to era. The European governmental system does it openly and clearly, and like it the Chinese governmental system, with much information absent; in a different style in the Japanese system, and less openly in the Russian, Canadian, American and Australian systems. All these governmental systems control, in one way or another, the course of life of "every individual" in society in return for securing the individual financially (suitable housing, a satisfying salary, suitable health care, suitable education, and fine services). No social welfare system can grant the citizen full, satisfying and comprehensive rights without controlling the citizen. There are systems that control without carrying a moral message; their control is for capitalist and security reasons only. As for control for the sake of realizing a real, human, moral message that makes the good, righteous human being who builds up the earth with true building, it is control of another kind, because it is control based on the responsibility of the governmental system toward the moral message, not toward the individual. Here the governmental system strengthens the push toward compulsory, not optional, awareness; toward binding, not optional, culture; and toward societal involvement in development, building and achievement by compulsion, not by choice; and toward making sure that every individual in society follows a righteous path, by monitoring behavior and correcting it through published laws and legislation, using "governmental" awareness pulpits to build a collective mind strictly committed to the moral vision of the governmental system, and drying up the sources of private media pulpits and eliminating them.""")

h(38, 400, """The Western governmental system controls the human being completely! Our countries too will do the same in the future!""")

h(38, 1400, """Forced return to the technology of the seventies: a sovereign decision that only an overwhelming superpower possesses!""")

t(38, 2100, """As if the internet, the smartphone, browsing, and being busy with entertainment all day are a pacifier made to distract! Not "as if"; it is the truth!

In the sixties of the twentieth century, the employee or worker would enter the company or factory in his youth and retire from it decades later, reassured about his future and his family's future. Wages rose steadily in line with increased productivity, which allowed an entire generation to buy houses and provide excellent education for their children from the income of "a single job."

Real purchasing power and the absence of high inflation
The sixties were marked by great stability in the prices of basic goods compared with the seventies and the present era. The purchasing power of the dollar and European currencies was very strong, and families did not suffer from rapid inflation that wipes out savings, as is the situation today.""")

t(39, 100, """What distinguished the sixties and the first half of the seventies was the absence of modern automation, the absence of globalization, every state in the world protecting its wealth and its products, agricultural or industrial, and the absence of the terrifying global economic entanglement that exists today.""")

h(39, 600, """The world economy today is 1000 times worse than in the sixties!""")

t(39, 1390, """This informational post is quoted "exclusively" for the sake of honesty .. Source: Google

Policies that facilitated moving factories and companies to developing countries (such as China, Mexico and Southeast Asia) in search of cheap labor led to the closure of thousands of factories locally:
In the United States
Free trade and outsourcing policies caused the loss of millions of industrial jobs, and economic studies point to what is known as the "China Shock" after its accession to the World Trade Organization in 2001, which caused the loss of about 2 to 2.4 million American jobs.

In Europe
Industrial countries such as Britain, France and Italy faced a wide wave of ending local manufacturing (Deindustrialization), which turned entire industrial cities into hotbeds of permanent unemployment, because traditional workers were unable to find alternatives.

The migration of middle-class and service jobs (the 21st century)

Unemployment resulting from international outsourcing was not limited to factory workers; with the development of the internet it extended to include middle-skill jobs in the service sector:
Western companies moved call centers (Call Centers), technical support services, accounting, data entry and software development to countries such as India and the Philippines.

This service outsourcing led to "polarization of the labor market" in America and Europe, as routine middle-class jobs disappeared and the market remained divided between high-wage, high-qualification jobs (such as top management and innovation) and very low-wage jobs (such as retail and cleaning).

The emergence of "structural unemployment" and the skills gap

Western government policies failed to create programs offering "rapid reskilling" (Reskilling) for workers who lost their jobs because of outsourcing:
The worker who spent decades in a steel or textile factory found himself unemployed and unable""")

t(40, 60, """to suddenly turn into a programmer or software engineer. This led to long-term structural unemployment, where modern technical jobs are available in the market but the unemployed do not have the skills needed to fill them.

Pressure on wages and the growth of the temporary economy: even for employees who did not lose their jobs completely, international outsourcing policies created a constant threat to them, as companies began threatening to move work abroad to pressure unions and local workers into accepting low wages or giving up job benefits, which led to the growth of unstable "temporary economy" (Gig Economy) jobs.""")

h(40, 900, """The systematic work done to wreck the West economically""")

t(40, 1600, """Automation (and later artificial intelligence) is the pivotal factor that changed the nature of jobs and unemployment rates between the seventies and the present era:

The destruction of routine jobs (intensive labor): in the seventies, a factory needed thousands of human workers on assembly lines. Today, thanks to robots and automation, the same factory can produce multiples of the quantity with a quarter of the number of workers, which shrank traditional job opportunities in the industrial sector.

The phenomenon of "job polarization":
Automation abolished middle-class jobs (middle-level and repetitive manual skills), and divided the current labor market into two groups: high-wage jobs for technology specialists, and low-wage jobs for physical services that the machine is still unable to do, which raised the rates of "structural unemployment."

The rapid skills gap:
In the seventies, the skill a worker learned was enough for him throughout his working life. Today, automation and artificial intelligence develop the work environment at insane speed, putting the skills of millions of people out of service within very few years.""")

