t(45, 345, """Much of our beautiful history is full of details of the lives of the Companions: how they lived, what their houses, streets and neighborhoods were like, their customs and traditions, their children and their ways of living. But we rarely speak of those very many details so as to draw close to them, understand them, and create a real human bond between us and them when we realize that they too were humans like us, with their daily affairs and their lives.

We have grown used to hearing only about the hardship of the Companions' living and how simple a life they had; the stereotyped image of them is that they owned nothing but mud houses, dates and water!

For example, the al-Aqiq neighborhood was, by today's standards, considered the neighborhood of the rich in Medina. The great Companion Umar ibn al-Khattab lived in the first half of the Prophetic era in the al-Awali neighborhood, 6 kilometers from Banu al-Najjar, where the Prophet ﷺ lived. Al-Faruq's house in al-Awali was inside his orchard, and he took care of his orchard; and he had a neighbor named Sulayt who had an orchard and a house beside al-Faruq's house. The al-Awali neighborhood was also considered of good quality compared with the Banu al-Najjar neighborhood, where the Seal of the Prophets, our Prophet ﷺ, lived among the modest mud houses. Even so, some families in Medina were wealthy, though not all of them:

The rich of the Ansar from al-Aws and al-Khazraj owned great wealth based on fertile farmland, palm and grape orchards, and sweet-water wells, unlike the rich of the Muhajirun (such as Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf), whose wealth was based on cash trade.
The most famous rich men of the Ansar can be classified as follows:

The rich of the Khazraj tribe
* Sa'd ibn Ubadah (chief of the Khazraj): He was the richest man among the Ansar without exception, famous for his legendary generosity. He had a large bowl (a food platter) that went around with the Prophet ﷺ to whichever of his houses he was in. Every day a call was made from atop his house: "Whoever wants fat and meat, let him come to the house of Sa'd." He equipped many of the military expeditions from his own wealth.

* Amr ibn al-Jamuh: He was one of the chiefs of Banu Salimah and one of their prominent rich men. He had an idol of his own in his house, made from the finest rare wood and coated with perfume, which reflects his luxury before Islam; then he devoted all his wealth to the service of the religion and was martyred at Uhud.

* Sa'd ibn al-Rabi': He was one of the great leaders and rich men of the Khazraj. He is the Companion whom the Prophet ﷺ made a brother of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, and he offered Ibn Awf to split his wealth and orchards with him in two halves, so great was his wealth.

* Abu al-Dahdah (Thabit ibn al-Dahdah): He owned a famous orchard in Medina with 600 fruit-bearing palms, a water well, and his house in which he lived. When the verse "Who is it that will lend Allah a goodly loan" was revealed, he gave the whole orchard in charity, and the Prophet ﷺ said: "How many heavy date clusters there are for Abu al-Dahdah in Paradise."

* Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah: He inherited his father's vast wealth and followed his way of lavish generosity, so that in the caliphate of Imam Ali he would lend thousands of dinars to those in hardship. When he fell ill, people were shy to visit him because of their many debts to him, so he ordered a crier to call out: "Whoever owes Qays a debt, he is released from it." By evening, the threshold of his door had broken from the great number of visitors.

The rich of the Aws tribe
* Sa'd ibn Mu'adh (chief of the Aws): He owned estates and wide farms in upper Medina (al-Awali), and was revered, rich, and listened to among his people; the Throne of the Most Merciful shook at his death.

* Al-Bara' ibn Ma'rur: He was a rich chief among his people. He was the first of the leaders to pledge allegiance at the Second Aqabah, and the first to bequeath a third of his wealth to the Prophet ﷺ to spend as he wished, before his death.

* Abu al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihan: He owned lush orchards full of palms and livestock. In a famous hadith, the Prophet ﷺ, Abu Bakr and Umar visited him while they were hungry, so he slaughtered a sheep from his flock for them and fed them fresh dates from his orchards.""")

h(46, 500, """Our history: the rich of the Ansar in the time of the Prophet ﷺ""")

t(46, 1210, """Alas .. there is salvation .. there is salvation .. for whoever grasps this noble verse .. Many people "took the devils as allies instead of Allah, and ((((they think)))) they are (((((guided))))" .. They think ... they think ... drowning in the illusion that they are right, as if they were being dragged to the Fire with their eyes closed! That is why you will find many doing satanic work complete in all its pillars while thinking they are doing godly work!""")

