t(48, 1000, """After his victory in the Battle of Hunayn, 6000 captives from Hawazin, Thaqif, Banu Hilal and Nasr were gathered with the Prophet ﷺ. So what did the Prophet ﷺ order his administration to do to deal with this huge number of captives?

Protecting 6,000 captives from the scorching desert sun in the valley of al-Ji'ranah was one of the astonishing humanitarian and organizational scenes in the Prophetic biography; they were not left in the open, but were treated with outstanding care through three specific logistical measures:

1. Building "shade enclosures" (collective shade)
As soon as the captives were gathered in the al-Ji'ranah area, Prophetic directives were issued to the army to build huge, temporary "enclosures."
Palm fronds, branches, hides, and the cloth available to the army were used to build these enclosures.
The historical reports (as found in the books of biography and military campaigns) mention that when the Prophet ﷺ arrived at al-Ji'ranah in the forenoon, he looked at those enclosures and was reassured about the captives' condition, and it was said to him: "O Messenger of Allah, these are the captives of Hawazin; they have been shaded from the sun."

2. Comprehensive clothing with the garments of Mecca (personal protection)
The captives' clothes had been torn or damaged during the battle, the sudden ambush, and the flight through the ravines, which left them exposed to the direct strikes of the sun on their bodies. To solve this problem, the Prophet ﷺ ordered the Companion Busr ibn Sufyan al-Khuza'i to head at once to the nearby markets of Mecca.

Busr bought enormous quantities of "mu'aqqad garments" (thick, wide garments made in Hajar and Yemen that withstand summer heat).
These garments were distributed to all 6,000 captives, and this clothing provided personal protection for the captives' bodies from sunburn.

3. Making use of the nature of the geography and the surrounding environment
The site of the camps was chosen in the heart of the valley of al-Ji'ranah, specifically in the "upper part of the valley."
This site was marked by the presence of samur trees and the natural shade of the surrounding rocks and mountains, which helped reduce the severity of the hot winds and provide additional natural shade areas for the captives throughout the waiting period, which lasted two weeks.""")

h(49, 300, """A huge logistical operation ordered by the Prophet ﷺ""")

